notes.understanding:
The radial-cell route to the band is exhausted, by a packing-free argument. With at most one cell per exceptional class, the frontier is at most 1/(3(1−η_max)), so crossing 1/3 by a definite amount needs a per-class saving η ≳ 1 − 1/(3h). That is an O(1) fraction of the host Weyl energy in a footprint of volume about V/k: the same diffuse multiclass state that P-positivity needs. The worthwhile band statement is qualitative: the fixed-complex Kähler frontier 1/3 is not the true α<1 frontier.

On Q4: on a closed almost-Kähler 4-manifold the Nijenhuis tensor N is a section of E = Λ^{0,2}⊗T^{1,0}. Then c₂(E) = c₂ + c₁(T)c₁(K⁻¹) + c₁² = χ + 2(2χ+3τ) = 5χ+6τ, which is exactly Armstrong's count. An AK-Einstein metric is real-analytic, so its zero set is analytic. Q4 therefore becomes the local structure of zeros of a section that solves an overdetermined system, plus a global Bochner balance. Candidate tools:
- the Sekigawa Weitzenböck identity, whose zeroth-order sign fails for s<0;
- the refined Kato inequality for first-order elliptic kernels (Calderbank–Gauduchon–Herzlich), which gains exactly at zeros;
- the index sign of an isolated zero, which would be positive if N satisfies a ∂̄-type equation modulo lower order;
- A01's localized Chern charge.
This is a constraint problem and fits A02's role; A01 holds construction and continuation.
